    "skills": {
      "s_39391f294d360026": {
        "k": "Phrase Grounding Verification for Conflicting Binary Choices When general VLMs and expert classifiers provide conflicting or negative results for a binary choice question regarding anatomical findings, switch to phrase grounding to visually verify the specific anatomical location of each option. Use the presence of a localized bounding box versus an empty result set as the decisive evidence for selection.",
        "c": {
          "name": "Phrase Grounding Verification for Conflicting Binary Choices",
          "description": "When general VLMs and expert classifiers provide conflicting or negative results for a binary choice question regarding anatomical findings, switch to phrase grounding to visually verify the specific anatomical location of each option. Use the presence of a localized bounding box versus an empty result set as the decisive evidence for selection.",
          "applicable_conditions": "Binary choice questions involving specific anatomical locations or findings where initial VLM/expert classifier outputs are conflicting, negative, or ambiguous.",
          "branch": "task_level",
          "tool_name": null,
          "task_category": "presence",
          "required_tools": [
            "xray_phrase_grounding"
          ],
          "risk_level": "medium",
          "core_action": "1. Identify the two competing options from the question.\n2. For each option, construct a specific phrase describing the finding and its location (e.g., 'right lower lobe atelectasis').\n3. Call xray_phrase_grounding for the first option's phrase.\n4. Check the observation: if predictions list is empty or analysis_status indicates no finding, record as negative.\n5. Call xray_phrase_grounding for the second option's phrase.\n6. Check the observation: if predictions contain valid bounding boxes, record as positive.\n7. Compare results: Select the option with valid grounding boxes. If both have boxes, prefer the one with higher confidence or more precise localization if available; if neither has boxes, re-evaluate or select based on prior probability if forced.\n8. Stop when a clear distinction in grounding presence/absence is established between the two options."
        },
        "u": 0.49396726872855923,
